WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:05.560
JOE: Dietary habits can be difficult
to understand and sometimes, to manage.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.709
They may involve different issues
such as eating only a few foods, only

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:14.750
eating at home, going long periods
without food or eating ‘nonfoods’

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.799
a condition known as ‘pica’.

00:00:19.129 --> 00:00:23.450
Many autistic people suffer with
obsessions, and if certain foods or

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:27.189
calorie counting, is their obsession,
this could lead to over-eating,

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.940
under-eating or a rigid diet.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.959
BETH: If an individual has problems
with overeating, reducing their

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.410
food portions, using a smaller plate
may help, or limiting the amount of

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.220
food readily available in the house.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.510
It’s a good idea to have a set timetable
for meal-times and setting limits around

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.390
visiting restaurants and take-aways.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.800
If an individual has under eating
problems, increasing food portions

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.800
or using a bigger plate may help,
as will increasing their contact

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.720
with foods of different varieties.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.660
JOE: Visual aids can also be used for
both problems showing foods that are

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.370
good and foods that are not so good,
and also showing visual representations

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.589
of hunger and fullness scales.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.809
BETH: Some individuals with
autism experience pica, which

00:01:13.809 --> 00:01:18.280
is eating or mouthing non-edible
items, such as stones and dirt.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.820
The reason a person on the autism spectrum
might experience pica, could be medical,

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.990
dietary, sensory or behavioural . They
may do this for a range of reasons

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:33.250
such as not understanding which items
are edible, liking the texture of

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:38.450
the item, to relieve pain or anxiety
or as a method of gaining attention.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:43.010
JOE: An obsession with eating, could
be channelled into something positive

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.359
such as cooking and writing recipes.

00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:49.379
Special interests could be used
to encourage them to eat more

00:01:49.529 --> 00:01:54.050
volume or variety, by cutting the
food into shapes or by looking at

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.470
foods from different countries.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.139
BETH: Many autistic people
rely on routine and sameness.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.720
They may need to have meals at the
same time every day, sit in the same

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:08.670
position at the table, or always use
a certain plate or bowl and cutlery.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:13.850
They may also like processed foods as
they are predictable, and look and taste

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.100
the same each time, whereas fresh food
or homemade food may vary in taste.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.610
JOE: It’s important to remember though,
that like all people, sometimes, obsessive

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.430
eating can be a coping strategy and
over-eating or avoiding food may be a

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.190
result of low self-esteem or anxiety.
